Disjoint Set Union treats every vertex as a separate group at first. When an edge joins two different groups, the groups are merged. If both ends are already in the same group, that edge gives a second connection between them and creates a cycle.

This also handles a self-loop because both ends immediately have the same representative. The trade-off is that the code does not use the adjacency links directly, except for scanning the edges stored in them.

```python
class Solution:
    def isCycle(self, V: int, adj: list[list[int]]) -> bool:
        parent = list(range(V))
        size = [1] * V

        def find(node: int) -> int:
            while parent[node] != node:
                parent[node] = parent[parent[node]]
                node = parent[node]
            return node

        def union(a: int, b: int) -> bool:
            root_a = find(a)
            root_b = find(b)

            if root_a == root_b:
                return False

            if size[root_a] < size[root_b]:
                root_a, root_b = root_b, root_a

            parent[root_b] = root_a
            size[root_a] += size[root_b]
            return True

        for node in range(V):
            for neighbor in adj[node]:
                if not union(node, neighbor):
                    return True

        return False
```

For a graph with no edges, every union is skipped and the answer is `false`. In a normal undirected adjacency list each edge appears twice, but the second scan is harmless because the first scan has already joined its endpoints.

Time complexity: `O((V + E) alpha(V))`, where `alpha` grows very slowly.
Space complexity: `O(V)`.

Here the recursive call is replaced with a stack. Each stack item stores a node and its parent, so the cycle check stays the same while avoiding JavaScript call-stack limits on long paths.

For a path `0-1-2`, the stack eventually processes `2` and finds only its parent `1`, so there is no cycle. If an extra edge `2-0` is present, `0` is already visited and is not the parent, which makes the answer `true`.

```javascript
class Solution {
    isCycle(V, adj) {
        const visited = new Array(V).fill(false);

        for (let start = 0; start < V; start++) {
            if (visited[start]) {
                continue;
            }

            const stack = [[start, -1]];
            visited[start] = true;

            while (stack.length > 0) {
                const [node, parent] = stack.pop();

                for (const next of adj[node]) {
                    if (!visited[next]) {
                        visited[next] = true;
                        stack.push([next, node]);
                    } else if (next !== parent) {
                        return true;
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```

Every component gets its own starting point. The method returns `false` for isolated vertices and for a tree, because every visited neighbor in a tree is just the parent.

Time complexity: `O(V + E)`.
Space complexity: `O(V)` for the visited array and stack.

This version uses BFS. Along with each queued vertex, it keeps the vertex from which that vertex was reached. If an already visited neighbor is not that parent, the edge closes a cycle.

A small dry run: starting at `0`, the queue may visit `1` and `2`. If `2` later sees `0`, `0` is already visited and is not `2`'s parent, so the method returns `true`.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public boolean isCycle(int V, List<Integer>[] adj) {
        boolean[] visited = new boolean[V];

        for (int start = 0; start < V; start++) {
            if (visited[start]) {
                continue;
            }

            Queue<int[]> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
            queue.offer(new int[]{start, -1});
            visited[start] = true;

            while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
                int[] current = queue.poll();
                int node = current[0];
                int parent = current[1];

                for (int next : adj[node]) {
                    if (!visited[next]) {
                        visited[next] = true;
                        queue.offer(new int[]{next, node});
                    } else if (next != parent) {
                        return true;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

The outer loop matters because the graph may have more than one connected component. BFS avoids recursion, which can be helpful for a deep graph.

Time complexity: `O(V + E)`.
Space complexity: `O(V)` for visited data and the queue.

A cycle has no leaf that can be removed forever. This gives another way to check the graph: repeatedly remove vertices with degree one. After all such removals, any vertex with a remaining edge belongs to a cycle.

The queue starts with all current leaves. Removing one leaf lowers the degree of its neighbors, and new leaves are added as soon as their degree becomes one. If nothing remains, the graph is a forest.

```python
from collections import deque

class Solution:
    def isCycle(self, V: int, adj: list[list[int]]) -> bool:
        degree = [len(neighbors) for neighbors in adj]
        queue = deque(node for node in range(V) if degree[node] == 1)
        removed = [False] * V

        while queue:
            node = queue.popleft()
            if removed[node]:
                continue

            removed[node] = True
            for neighbor in adj[node]:
                if removed[neighbor]:
                    continue

                degree[neighbor] -= 1
                if degree[neighbor] == 1:
                    queue.append(neighbor)

        return any(not removed[node] and degree[node] > 0 for node in range(V))
```

An isolated vertex has degree zero and is not put into the queue, but it also cannot make a cycle. This method assumes the usual undirected adjacency list, where an edge is listed for both endpoints.

Time complexity: `O(V + E)`.
Space complexity: `O(V)` for degrees, removal marks, and the queue.

A parent node is enough to tell a real back edge from the edge that simply leads back to the node we came from. The DFS starts again for every unvisited vertex, so disconnected parts are covered too.

For example, with edges `0-1`, `1-2`, and `2-0`, DFS reaches `2` and sees that `0` is already visited and is not its parent. That confirms a cycle.

```cpp
class Solution{
    bool dfs(int node, int parent, vector<int> adj[], vector<bool>& visited) {
        visited[node] = true;

        for (int next : adj[node]) {
            if (!visited[next]) {
                if (dfs(next, node, adj, visited)) {
                    return true;
                }
            } else if (next != parent) {
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }

public:
    bool isCycle(int V, vector<int> adj[]) {
        vector<bool> visited(V, false);

        for (int node = 0; node < V; node++) {
            if (!visited[node] && dfs(node, -1, adj, visited)) {
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }
};
```

An empty graph or a graph with only isolated vertices returns `false`. The recursive depth can become large on a long chain.

Time complexity: `O(V + E)`.
Space complexity: `O(V)` for the visited array and recursion stack.

